foreign  [Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Wednesday November 2nd I'm Julie Chang  for the Wall Street Journal filling in  for Zoe Thomas  a lot of us have well streaming service  fatigue subscription after a  subscription Netflix Hulu Disney plus  the list goes on  YouTube thinks it can tackle that  fatigue by selling some of those  subscriptions itself the new feature  called Prime Time channels launched this  week and it lets users buy over 30  channels like Paramount plus and  Showtime directly on YouTube the  backdrop to all of this is that YouTube  last week reported its first drop in  advertising Revenue since it started  breaking out the unit's performance  parent company Google is dealing with  its own challenges reporting its fifth  consecutive quarter of slowing sales  growth so how might this feature help  the video platform Giant and how might  this impact streaming service providers  here to answer those questions and more  is wsj reporter miles Krupa miles thank  you for coming on the show thanks for  having me so Miles YouTube ruled out an  early version of its new streaming  service store can you tell us more about  it what does it look like  sure so the idea is that these different  streaming services including Paramount  plus Showtime Stars they will all sort  of have their own page kind of like a  YouTube Creator would and the idea  really is to have these streaming  providers have their content seamlessly  slot in between the user generated  content that people know and love from  YouTube so you know one example they  keep repeating is perhaps you're  watching a trailer for a hit show on  Showtime and then because of the way  these Services work together in  YouTube's recommendation algorithms  ideally the service will then point you  to a way to subscribe to Showtime  directly through YouTube and why did  YouTube launch this new Marketplace  yeah so YouTube's big ambition which  it's largely fulfilled already is to  basically be a One-Stop shop if you want  to watch a video of any kind on any  screen so YouTube already had something  called YouTube TV which is like a Cable  Bundle it's almost 65 a month and then  of course it has things like short form  video the long form content what this is  is kind of like an in-between it's more  what they call an A La Carte option so  for people who might not want to Shell  out 65 a month for YouTube TV they can  pick and choose a few different Services  here and there and it's another way for  YouTube to capture the push towards  online streaming but at a lower dollar  cost for the consumer so ultimately  YouTube will split the subscription and  advertising revenue from these streaming  service is with the partners themselves  YouTube wouldn't tell me exactly how  that split will break down but fair to  say YouTube could be capturing probably  at least a quarter of the revenue from  these subscribers okay so that's what  YouTube gets out of it but what about  other streaming companies or  entertainment companies what would they  get out of this agreement  yeah with the broader push towards  online streaming there are a lot of  older entertainment companies that are  trying to catch up to the Netflix's and  Hulu's of the world who are kind of  coming to this a little bit late and  don't quite have the same brand name  recognition or distribution and this is  really an easy way for them to get in  front of eyeballs YouTube has more than  2 billion logged in users and if you can  get one of them to click on a Paramount  Plus subscription after watching  something related on YouTube even if  you're giving up a good amount of that  subscription Revenue that's a win if  you're sort of a second or third tier  subscription streaming service provider  couldn't other streaming services like  Netflix just offer up something similar  so Amazon and apple and Roku are  probably the best comparisons Amazon  allows you to add on individual channels  to your Prime subscription Apple TV plus  offers a sort of similar store Front for  different streaming services so it is a  crowded space YouTube so far hasn't  signed any really exclusive streaming  providers to come onto the service which  could be a difficulty but what  executives are really banking on is the  reach of YouTube  immediately allowing this to become a  big business for them what does this  move by YouTube tell us about where the  streaming industry is headed yeah the  streaming industry is in a bit of a  transformation period right now people  seem more confused than ever they might  be subscribed to different services on  different platforms and not truly  understand how much they're paying for  all the different streaming services so  I think you're seeing a few things  you're seeing some people try to offer  bundles like what Disney is doing with  Disney Plus Hulu and ESPN plus and then  you're also now seeing platforms like  YouTube saying we want to give consumers  more Choice while also trying to have  consumers be captive to YouTube's  platform right maybe it's more choice  but also we would love for you to  subscribe to all of your services  through YouTube so  they're sort of a few different ways  this shakes out and I think YouTube is  basically betting that there's a class  of consumers that wants  these Individual Services at a lower  cost and they want them all in one place  if you win it all you can get YouTube TV  for 65 a month but if you want something  that's a bit lower commitment all in one  place then you have Prime Time channels  all right miles we're gonna have to  leave it there miles Krupa reporter with  the Wall Street Journal thank you so  much for being here today thank you and  that's it for today's Tech news briefing  for more Tech stories check out our  website wsj.com I'm Julie Chang for the  Wall Street Journal thanks for listening  [Music]  foreign

